# Caddy Reverse Proxy Configuration - Security Hardened
# Includes: HSTS, CSP, security headers, real rate limiting, auto-HTTPS
#
# Rate limiting uses the mholt/caddy-ratelimit plugin, compiled into the
# image via ./Dockerfile (the stock caddy image does not include it).
# {$DOMAIN} and {$QBIT_PORT} are substituted from the container's
# environment (see docker-compose.yml) at config-load time.
#
# Behind Cloudflare Tunnel (see the cloudflared service in
# docker-compose.yml), this server has no inbound port 80/443 access from
# the internet at all - Starlink's CGNAT makes that impossible anyway.
# acme_dns switches ALL certificate issuance from the old HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN-01
# challenges (which need an inbound request) to DNS-01 (proves domain
# ownership via a DNS TXT record instead) - this one global directive
# applies to every site block below with no further per-site changes,
# via the caddy-dns/cloudflare plugin compiled in by ./Dockerfile. The
# token needs Zone:DNS:Edit permission for this domain only - see
# SETUP.md for how to create it.
#
# tls_resolvers is required on this host specifically - see
# TROUBLESHOOTING.md ("DNS-01 propagation checks fail: outbound TCP port
# 53 is blocked by ProtonVPN's DNS leak protection"). ProtonVPN's DNS
# leak protection blocks outbound TCP port 53 system-wide (including
# Docker/WSL2 traffic) to everything except its own tunnel resolver -
# certmagic's zone-detection step (finding the SOA record) tries UDP
# first but falls back to TCP on timeout, and that fallback is what gets
# blocked. Pointing tls_resolvers at ProtonVPN's own resolver sidesteps
# this entirely, since it's a real general-purpose recursive resolver
# (not restricted to VPN-internal domains) and TCP to it isn't blocked.
# This IP is specific to this ProtonVPN client's current tunnel - if it
# changes (reconnect, different server), check the "ProtonVPN" network
# adapter's assigned DNS server (ipconfig /all) and update this.
#
# 1.1.1.1 is the fallback for when the host's ProtonVPN is disconnected
# (10.2.0.1 then times out - seen live after a reboot left ProtonVPN off).
# Zone detection tries each resolver in order, but certmagic's
# propagation check queries the first one only and fails outright, so
# that check is skipped (propagation_timeout -1) in favour of a fixed
# wait: Cloudflare publishes the TXT record within seconds, and Let's
# Encrypt retries validation itself. This is a global cert_issuer rather
# than acme_ca/acme_dns/tls_resolvers because only the issuer block
# accepts the propagation options - and it covers renewals of every
# existing cert, not just new sites.

# Global settings
{
  email {$ACME_EMAIL}
  cert_issuer acme https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory {
    dns cloudflare {$CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
    resolvers 10.2.0.1 1.1.1.1
    propagation_delay 60s
    propagation_timeout -1
  }

  order rate_limit after basic_auth
  # Wake on-demand containers before proxying to them (see Sablier below).
  order sablier before reverse_proxy

  # Collect per-host HTTP metrics (requests, latency, status codes) for
  # Prometheus - served by the :9180 site at the bottom of this file.
  metrics {
    per_host
  }

  # Without this, every request Caddy sees - whether it came through
  # cloudflared or hit the host's own 80/443 directly - looks like it
  # came from cloudflared's own container IP, because that's the actual
  # TCP peer for tunnel traffic (cloudflared terminates the tunnel, then
  # proxies to Caddy over caddy-network). Confirmed live before this fix:
  # {http.request.remote} was cloudflared's IP (172.20.0.3) for every
  # single visitor, no matter their real IP. That silently broke three
  # things at once - rate limiting's per-client key (shared across every
  # real visitor instead of actually per-client), every X-Real-IP header
  # forwarded to backend apps (their own login-attempt/ban logic saw the
  # same wrong shared IP for everyone), and Fail2Ban (even if it could
  # ban on this host - see TROUBLESHOOTING.md - it would have banned
  # cloudflared's IP, taking down the site for everyone).
  # 172.20.0.0/16 is caddy-network's actual subnet (confirmed via
  # `docker network inspect caddy-network`) - trusting it specifically,
  # not a blanket range, means only cloudflared's traffic gets its
  # forwarded header trusted; a LAN client hitting 80/443 directly still
  # gets treated as itself, correctly, with nothing to unwrap.
  # trusted_proxies is a `servers` sub-option, not top-level - confirmed
  # live: Caddy rejects it as "unrecognized global option" at the top
  # level (caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/options lists it under
  # "Server Options", inside `servers { ... }`).
  servers {
    trusted_proxies static 172.20.0.0/16
  }

  # CrowdSec bouncer (optional) - enforces crowdsec-stack's ban decisions
  # in Caddy itself. The glob matches nothing on a deployment without
  # CrowdSec (Caddy only warns on an empty glob), so this stays inert
  # until _scripts/enable-crowdsec.ps1 writes crowdsec.d/global.caddy and
  # crowdsec.d/handler.caddy from the committed .example templates (the
  # real ones hold the bouncer's LAPI key and are gitignored). The
  # handler half is imported by (security_headers) below, so every site
  # gets it. Relies on trusted_proxies above to see the real client IP.
  import /etc/caddy/crowdsec.d/global*.caddy

  # No `log` directive existed anywhere in this file before - Caddy was
  # producing zero access logs for actual site traffic (only its own
  # internal admin-API healthcheck hits were ever logged). This is the
  # default logger every site block below inherits unless it sets its
  # own; written to the same named volume Caddy already uses for TLS
  # state, so no new mount was needed.
  log {
    output file /data/access.log {
      roll_size 50mb
      roll_keep 5
    }
    format json
  }
}

# Security header middleware (applied to all routes)
(security_headers) {
  # CrowdSec ban check - no-op unless enabled, see the global block.
  import /etc/caddy/crowdsec.d/handler*.caddy

  # HTTPS Strict Transport Security (force HTTPS for 1 year)
  header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload"

  # Prevent MIME sniffing
  header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"

  # Prevent clickjacking
  header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"

  # XSS Protection (deprecated but good fallback)
  header X-XSS-Protection "1; mode=block"

  # Content Security Policy (prevent injection attacks)
  header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https:;"

  # Referrer Policy (privacy)
  header Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"

  # Permissions Policy (disable dangerous features)
  header Permissions-Policy "accelerometer=(), camera=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()"

  # Remove X-Powered-By (don't reveal tech stack)
  header -Server
}

# Rate limiting middleware (defense-in-depth against abuse/DoS - actual
# login brute-forcing is already stopped by basic_auth, which runs BEFORE
# this per the "order rate_limit after basic_auth" directive above, so a
# bad password never even reaches this counter).
# Each call site passes a unique zone name (import rate_limit <name>) so
# multiple site blocks don't collide on one shared zone definition.
# events was originally 30/min, which sounds generous until you remember
# these are all SPA dashboards: Sonarr/Radarr/Prowlarr/Lidarr each fire
# 15-30+ API calls just loading the status page, and qBittorrent/Seerr
# poll every few seconds for live queue/activity updates. Confirmed live
# via Caddy's own logs ("rate limit exceeded", zone sonarr_zone) that normal
# single-user browsing was tripping this within seconds of opening a tab,
# surfacing to the user as "Failed to load system status from API" -
# indistinguishable from the app actually being down. Raised well above
# realistic dashboard-polling volume while still bounding runaway abuse.
(rate_limit) {
  rate_limit {
    zone {args[0]}_zone {
      key    {remote_host}
      events 600
      window 1m
    }
  }
}

# Sonarr/Radarr/Prowlarr/Lidarr and qBittorrent each have their own real,
# mandatory login (Settings > General > Security, AuthenticationRequired
# "Enabled" - confirmed live, not the "disabled for local addresses"
# default some of these apps ship with, which WOULD have been meaningless
# behind a reverse proxy since every request looks local to the app).
# arr_auth/qbit_auth basic_auth snippets used to duplicate that with a
# second, Caddy-level login - removed deliberately: it added no real
# protection once the app's own login was confirmed genuinely enforced,
# and HTTP Basic's browser-native prompt doesn't autofill from a password
# manager the way an app's own HTML login form does, which was pure
# friction for zero benefit. Each app now gets exactly one login: its
# own. See TROUBLESHOOTING.md for the qBittorrent self-ban issue this
# same basic_auth layer caused, which is also moot now that it's gone.
#
# Those admin apps (and Syncthing, Uptime Kuma, Pi-hole, slskd) now sit
# behind the `authentik` snippet below instead. It's an SSO wall rather
# than a second password prompt: one Authentik sign-in (password manager
# friendly, MFA-capable) covers every admin subdomain for 24h, and only
# "authentik Admins" get through (authentik/blueprints/admin-apps.yaml).
#
# Usage: `import authentik [exempt paths...]`. List each app's API paths
# as exempt - they're already guarded by the app's API key, and Homepage
# widgets and phone apps (nzb360, LunaSea...) can't do a browser login.
(authentik) {
  # Authentik's sign-in callback and assets, served for every protected site.
  reverse_proxy /outpost.goauthentik.io/* authentik-server:9000
  @authentik_required not path /outpost.goauthentik.io/* {args[:]}
  forward_auth @authentik_required authentik-server:9000 {
    uri /outpost.goauthentik.io/auth/caddy
    copy_headers X-Authentik-Username X-Authentik-Groups X-Authentik-Email X-Authentik-Name X-Authentik-Uid
    trusted_proxies private_ranges
  }
}

